WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.696
When you inspect a packet, read it as a stack of layers.

2
00:00:03.696 --> 00:00:12.404
Start with the frame information, then move through link, network, transport, and application layers when they are present.

3
00:00:12.404 --> 00:00:23.083
In many beginner captures, you will see Ethernet, IP, TCP or UDP, and then a higher-level protocol such as DNS, HTTP, TLS, or another application protocol.

4
00:00:23.083 --> 00:00:26.615
Source and destination fields tell you direction.

5
00:00:26.615 --> 00:00:35.158
At the IP layer, the source address is the system that sent the packet and the destination address is the system it was sent to.

6
00:00:35.158 --> 00:00:41.072
At the TCP or UDP layer, ports help identify the service or application conversation.

7
00:00:41.072 --> 00:00:51.258
A destination port of 443 commonly suggests encrypted web traffic, but remember that ports are clues, not absolute proof of application behavior.

8
00:00:51.258 --> 00:01:00.047
Protocol fields give more detail. In TCP, flags help show whether a connection is being established, acknowledged, or closed.

9
00:01:00.047 --> 00:01:10.315
Sequence and acknowledgment values help Wireshark track order and reliability. Retransmissions may suggest loss or delay, but they need context.

10
00:01:10.315 --> 00:01:17.051
In DNS, queries and responses show names, record types, response codes, and returned addresses.

11
00:01:17.051 --> 00:01:25.019
In HTTP, if visible in an approved lab capture, requests and response codes can help explain application behavior.

12
00:01:25.019 --> 00:01:28.551
Avoid building a conclusion from one packet alone.

13
00:01:28.551 --> 00:01:36.273
One SYN packet can show that a connection attempt was made, but the next packets tell you whether it was answered.

14
00:01:36.273 --> 00:01:44.159
One DNS response can show a returned address, but related queries may show retries, failures, or alternate names.

15
00:01:44.159 --> 00:01:49.498
One error-looking field may be normal for that protocol or that environment.

16
00:01:49.498 --> 00:01:59.684
A careful analyst compares related packets, checks timestamps, and separates observation from interpretation. Say what the packet shows first.

17
00:01:59.684 --> 00:02:05.023
Then explain what it may mean. If you are not sure, write down the uncertainty.

18
00:02:05.023 --> 00:02:09.788
Packet fields are precise, but conclusions still depend on context.
